 "drivingQuestion": "The mishna implies excess frankincense is fit; the baraita says it is disqualified — how to reconcile?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Rami bar Hama limits the baraita to a case of two handfuls",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "menachot.11b.8",
   "daf": "11b",
   "sentenceId": 7,
   "he": "חִיסֵּר לְבוֹנָתָהּ – הָא יָתֵיר כְּשֵׁרָה, וְהָתַנְיָא: יָתֵיר פְּסוּלָה! אָמַר רָמֵי בַּר חָמָא: כְּגוֹן שֶׁהִפְרִישׁ לָהּ שְׁנֵי קְמָצִין.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches that if the priest «decreased its frankincense» beyond its appropriate measure, the meal offering is unfit. The Gemara infers from this statement that if «he increased» its frankincense, it is «fit.» The Gemara asks: «But isn’t it taught» in a baraita that if he «increased» its frankincense the meal offering is «disqualified? Rami bar Ḥama said:» The baraita rules that the meal offering is disqualified in a case «where he separated two handfuls» of frankincense «for» the meal offering and placed both of them onto the meal offering."
  }
 ],
